
  Show what the jobs of the worktrees named printed: each job's last lines (1000 at most), from
  its log, whether it runs, ended or crashed. --job reads one job alone.

  At a terminal it opens the live view on their jobs, after the worktree picker when none is
  named (the one you stand in picked; --yes skips the picker for it). Leaving the view stops
  nothing. Anywhere else (no terminal, --quiet, --output json) it reads the worktree you stand
  in unless named, prints each job's lines as `[job] text` and ends; --follow (-f) then prints
  each new line as the jobs print it, until they all ended or Ctrl+C, which exits 0.

  --output json is the read's v2 envelope, each job with its state and its lines; with
  --follow, one typed JSON object per line instead: line, absent, warning, error.
  It never starts the run daemon: with none running, the lines come from the logs.

  Usage:
    wtm-next run logs [worktree...] [flags]

  Examples:
    # Pick worktrees, then watch their jobs live
    wtm run logs

    # One job's last lines, for a script or an agent
    wtm run logs feat/login --job api --output json

    # Follow a job's output without a terminal
    wtm run logs feat/login --job api --follow | grep ERROR

  Flags:
    -f, --follow          Print each new line as the jobs print it, until they end or Ctrl+C
    -h, --help            help for logs
        --job string      Read this job alone, one of run.toml or one the run daemon runs there (default: every job that ran)
        --output string   Output format: text or json (default "text")
    -y, --yes             Ask nothing: read the worktree you stand in unless named

  Global Flags:
    -q, --quiet   Silence human output; errors and the exit code are unaffected, and --output json still emits its document

